# The Smallest Livelit That Needs Splices #

# A splice is a region of the CLIENT's program that sits inside a #
# widget: an editor for ordinary code, in the client's scope #
# (Sec. 2.4.2, 2.4.3). This is the smallest livelit that needs one #
# -- a slider whose two ends are the client's code. (The paper's own #
# $slider takes its bounds as parameters, Sec. 2.4.1, written outside #
# the widget. Here they are splices, edited inside it.) #

# This slide runs a STOPGAP, not the paper's design. In the paper #
# (Figure 3, Sec. 3.2) a livelit MAKES its splices with new_splice #
# and WRITES them with set_splice, both UpdateCmds. Only the view #
# READS one, with eval_splice, and places its editor with editor, #
# both ViewCmds. expand writes code for a function over them. Those #
# commands typecheck here, but only editor runs: there is no splice #
# store yet. So the stopgap uses what the editor already has. Parens #
# around #
# the whole value of a field in a use's model make that field a #
# splice, and each spliced field carries its value beside its ref. #

# Once the store exists, this slide's code is to be rewritten against #
# the paper's commands. Until then the comments say which part is the #
# paper's design and which is the stopgap; THE STOPGAP BESIDE THE #
# PAPER, at the end, sets the two side by side. #

# WHAT RUNS TODAY #

# Here is a slider reporting a number between two bounds. Drag it. #

let ^between = {
  # pct is where the handle sits, 0 to 100. lo and hi are the ends of #
  # the range. #
  # In Figure 3 a spliced field is only a HANDLE, a SpliceRef (l.3-4), #
  # and the view evaluates the code behind it with eval_splice, under #
  # the closure the reader has selected (Sec. 3.2.3). Here each bound #
  # also carries its value. That is the stopgap: eval_splice does not #
  # run, so the value rides in the model instead. #
  type Model = (
    pct=Int,
    lo=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int),
    hi=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int)
  );

  # Only the handle moves: there is no SetLo or SetHi. The bounds are #
  # the client's code, and this slider leaves them alone -- though the #
  # paper does let update rewrite a splice; see WHY THE BOUNDS ARE #
  # SPLICES below. #
  type Action = + Slide(Int);

  type Expansion = Int;


  # init is plain data, pasted as text at the caret when you type #
  # ^between. The parens are the STOPGAP's splice marker: once pasted, #
  # lo=(0) makes lo a splice whose code starts as 0. The paper makes #
  # each splice with new_splice instead, inside an init that is a #
  # command (Sec. 3.2.1); the new_splice section below says why ours #
  # is not. init supplies VALUES; only a use supplies the REFS, once #
  # its parens have been read as splices. So init is checked at each #
  # spliced field's value type, Int, not at the (ref, value) pair. #
  # That is also why it has no `: Model`, which would ask for pairs. #
  let init = (pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(100));

  let update = fun m : Model -> fun a : Action -> Pure(
    case a
    | Slide(p) => (pct=p, lo=m.lo, hi=m.hi)
    end);

  # Where the handle lands, in the client's units. #
  let at = fun m : Model ->
    m.lo.value + (m.hi.value - m.lo.value) * m.pct / 100;

  let view = fun m : Model -> Pure(
    Html.div(
      [Attr.style([("display", "flex"), ("gap", "0.4em"),
                   ("align-items", "center"),
                   ("font-family", "var(--code-font)"),
                   ("font-size", "0.8em")])],
      # Each splice's editor is placed by the ref its field carries. #
      # The paper does this with the ViewCmd editor((ref, dim)) #
      # (Sec. 3.2.3); Html.splice(ref) is the stopgap that runs today. #
      # They are real editors: click in and type. What you type is #
      # code in YOUR scope, not in the livelit's (Sec. 2.4.3). #
      [Html.splice(m.lo.ref),
       Html.input(
         [Attr.type_("range"), Attr.min("0"), Attr.max("100"),
          Attr.value(string_of_int(m.pct)),
          Attr.on_input(fun s -> Slide(int_of_string(s)))]),
       Html.splice(m.hi.ref),
       Html.text(" = " ++ string_of_int(at(m)))]));

  let expand = Functional(fun m : Model -> at(m));

  # Width of the widget's OWN chrome only -- the slider and the #
  # " = N". The splices add their own width on top, because only the #
  # editor knows how long the client's code is. Counting them here #
  # too double-reserves and the widget sits in a wide empty box. 22 #
  # was too tight and clipped the " = N"; 26 leaves room for a #
  # three-digit result. #
  let shape = Inline(26)
} in

# WHY THE BOUNDS ARE SPLICES #

# In the paper a model is plain data: it is what gets saved, so it can #
# hold no functions (Sec. 3.2.1). The client's code reaches a widget #
# only through its parameters and splices (Sec. 2.4). #

# Here a use's model argument happens to be an ordinary expression in #
# the client's scope, so a plain Int field could say `baseline * 2` #
# and it would evaluate -- until the first drag. An interaction #
# commits the model VALUE back into the text, and the field would #
# then read 20. Without splices a livelit could COMPUTE with the #
# client's code; it could not HOLD any. #

# A splice holds it. On commit a spliced field keeps the client's #
# term, not its value, and the widget shows that term where the author #
# chose, as a real editor, beside whatever the author puts next to it. #
# The code inside stays the client's, in the client's scope #
# (Sec. 2.4.3). #

# A livelit may still REWRITE a splice. That is set_splice, and #
# Figure 3's update uses it to overwrite all four channels on a click #
# (Sec. 3.2.4). This slider has no reason to. Nor could it yet: #
# set_splice's code is an Exp, Exp has no inhabitants, and the #
# command does not run. #

# READING AND SHOWING #

# Note what `at` does. It reads m.lo.value and m.hi.value, and both #
# view and expand call it, while the view places m.lo.ref and #
# m.hi.ref. In the paper only the view evaluates a splice, with the #
# ViewCmd eval_splice, and places it with editor (Sec. 3.2.3). expand #
# never sees a value and treats the splices parametrically #
# (Sec. 3.2.5). Here the value rides in the model as the stopgap for #
# eval_splice, so expand reads it too. THE MODEL and THE EXPANSION, #
# below, say what that costs. #

let baseline = 10 in

let shown = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(baseline), hi=(baseline * 2)))) in

# SPLICES COMPOSE #

# A splice holds an EXPRESSION, and a use of a livelit is an #
# expression, so one of these can be the bound of another #
# (Sec. 2.4.2). Three ways, and the difference between them is only #
# where you write it. #

# 1. BY NAME. The inner slider's value is the outer slider's maximum. #

let cap = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200)))) in
let by_name = ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(cap)))) in

# 2. NESTED IN PLACE. The same relationship, with the inner slider #
# living INSIDE the outer one's max splice. Drag the inner handle and #
# let go, and the outer slider's range changes under you. (While you #
# drag, only the inner widget redraws; the release commits.) #

let nested =
  ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0),
    hi=(^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200)))))))) in

# 3. COMPUTED. A splice is not a slot for a widget, it is a region of #
# your program -- so the maximum can be arithmetic over one. #

let computed =
  ^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0),
    hi=((^^livelit(^between((pct=50, lo=(0), hi=(200))))) * 2 + 10)))) in

# Watch where the parens close in 3. The stopgap's rule: a field #
# becomes a splice only when its WHOLE value, spaces aside, is one #
# parenthesized group. Written `hi=(slider) * 2 + 10`, the parens #
# hold only part of the value, so the stopgap does not read them as #
# a marker at all: no splice is made, the field has no ref, and it #
# does not typecheck. In the paper the client marks nothing: the #
# livelit makes each splice itself, with new_splice (Sec. 3.2.1). #

(shown, by_name, nested, computed)

# Try: change baseline to 20 and watch both bounds follow. Or type #
# `baseline * 3` into the right-hand splice. Or empty a bound and see #
# what the widget does with a hole. #

# ============ THE STOPGAP BESIDE THE PAPER ============ #

# The slider above runs on the stopgap. Here is the paper's design #
# beside it, piece by piece (Omar et al., PLDI 2021, Figure 3). It is #
# also the shape this slide's code will take once the store exists. #

# THE MODEL: REFS, NOT VALUES, IN THE PAPER #

# Figure 3's model holds splice refs, never their values (l.3-4). #
# For this slider that would be: #
#   type Model = (pct=Int, lo=SpliceRef, hi=SpliceRef) #

# Here each bound is a pair: #
#   lo=(ref=SpliceRef, value=Int) #

# The ref is what `view` names. The VALUE riding alongside is the #
# stopgap for eval_splice, and it costs two things here. (A third, #
# that expand sees it, is under THE EXPANSION.) #

# First, update is handed the value too. The paper keeps eval_splice #
# out of UpdateCmd so that the model does not depend DIRECTLY on the #
# selected closure; a view may still pass a result to update, on #
# purpose, inside an action (Sec. 3.2.4). Here that dependence is #
# built in on every update, not opted into by the view. #

# Second, there is no way to say a bound has NO value. eval_splice #
# answers Maybe(Result): None when there is nothing to evaluate it #
# under, and otherwise Val(v), or Indet for an expression held up by #
# a hole (Sec. 3.2.3, Sec. 2.5.2). The widget then decides what to #
# show. Delete a bound here and the widget cannot decide anything -- #
# the result simply renders as a hole, because nothing asked it. #

# new_splice TYPECHECKS, BUT DOES NOT RUN #

#   new_splice : (Typ, Maybe(Exp)) -> UpdateCmd(SpliceRef) #

# The paper MAKES a splice as an effect, at a declared type, with #
# optional starting code (Sec. 3.2.1). UpdateCmd declares the same #
# command here, and its Typ is a stopgap closed sum -- IntT, FloatT, #
# BoolT, StringT -- standing in for the paper's quoted types. But #
# there is no splice store behind it, so running it reports "not #
# implemented". So does every splice command except editor, which #
# only places a splice the widget already has. Deletion is implicit, #
# with no drop_splice: a splice that #
# nothing in the model refers to is simply garbage. #

# Until new_splice runs, the stopgap recovers splices from the program #
# text on every load, by the parens the author wrote. Nothing types #
# such a splice at a declared type; it is typed only by where it sits #
# in the model tuple. For `between` that gives the right answer, Int, #
# but by position, not by a mechanism. #

# The paper's init is itself an UpdateCmd, so it can make the first #
# splices (Sec. 3.2.1). Ours is a plain Model, and would stay one even #
# if new_splice ran: init is pasted as text at the caret when you type #
# the name, and at that moment there is no evaluator to run a #
# command. #

# The other consequence: until new_splice runs, the number of splices #
# is fixed by the text, where the paper lets it change as the user #
# interacts (Sec. 2.4.2). An update that makes one already typechecks, #
#   do r <- new_splice((IntT, None)) in Pure(...) #
# but running it reports "not implemented". So an editable LIST of #
# splices can be written, not run; Splices, Dynamically (draft) is #
# that slide. #

# THE EXPANSION: A FUNCTION OF THE SPLICES #

#   Macro( Model -> (Exp, List(SpliceRef)) ) #

# The paper's expand writes a PROGRAM: quoted code for a function #
# (curried, in Figure 3) taking one argument per SpliceRef in the #
# list it is handed back with -- splices, and parameters too #
# (Sec. 3.2.5). For this slider: #

#   (`fun lo hi -> lo + (hi - lo) * pct / 100`, [m.lo, m.hi]) #

# with pct standing for the model's number, written into the code. #

# That shape is not decoration. It is how the paper gets CAPTURE #
# AVOIDANCE: a splice passed in as an argument is scoped at the use, #
# so no binding inside the expansion can capture it (Sec. 2.4.3, #
# Sec. 3.2.5). #

# Here Macro can be written but cannot return real code: there is no #
# quotation, so its Exp can only be a hole. This slider uses the #
# Functional arm instead, this implementation's addition. It returns #
# a VALUE, from bounds already evaluated in the client's scope -- the #
# values riding in the model. Capture cannot arise because nothing is #
# substituted into a binder -- but equally, nothing here demonstrates #
# the paper's solution. We avoided the problem rather than solving it. #

# WHAT WOULD BREAK FIRST #

# Two known walls in the stopgap, in the order you would hit them: #

#   A splice itself does not survive a save/load round trip -- it #
#   prints as nothing but its content. Only the parens survive, which #
#   is why they are the marker: on every load the livelit projector #
#   (not the livelit's own init) rebuilds the splices from them. #

#   Committing a model rebuilds the zipper from the ROOT, so a widget #
#   action destroys the editor the caret was sitting in. Nothing about #
#   a splice survives that on its own; focus is put back afterwards, #
#   explicitly, by matching the splice id #
#   (FocusEffect.keep_splice_focus). That restoration is not #
#   complete -- it is a patch over a rebuild, not a caret the #
#   rebuild preserves. #

# Both come from building the stopgap out of the editor's existing #
# machinery instead of the splice store the paper's commands assume. #
